The smallest root drifts toward $0$ as $n$ grows, explaining why no fixed integer ladder suffices for all $n$.

\section{Algorithms and exact verification}
All numerical claims can be verified with exact arithmetic.

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Algorithm A (generation).} Compute $A(n,k)$ by memoized recurrence, then $C(n,k)=\sum_j A(n,j)A(j,k)$ and the coefficients of $B_n$; $O(n^2)$ big-integer operations per row.
\item \textbf{Algorithm B (identity check).} Independently form $\sum_j A(n,j)A_j$ by polynomial addition/scaling and compare with $B_n$ coefficientwise, certifying Theorem~\ref{thm:struct}.
\item \textbf{Algorithm C (positivity).} Confirm all coefficients of $B_n$ are $\ge0$ with constant term $n!>0$, establishing Lemma~\ref{lem:pos}.
\item \textbf{Algorithm D (Sturm real-root count).} Build the Sturm chain $p_0=B_n$, $p_1=B_n'$, $p_{i+1}=-\rem(p_{i-1},p_i)$ over $\Q$; for rational $a<b$ outside a Cauchy root bound, the number of distinct real roots is $V(a)-V(b)$, where $V(t)$ counts sign changes in $(p_0(t),p_1(t),\dots)$. Equality with $\deg B_n$ certifies real-rootedness exactly.
\end{itemize}

Running A--D for $0\le n\le10$ confirms the structural identity, nonnegativity with constant term $n!$, exactly $\deg B_n$ distinct real roots for each $n$, and the $(-1,0)$ root counts $0$ ($n\le4$), $1$ ($5\le n\le7$), $2$ ($8\le n\le10$).

\section{Discussion and future work}

\paragraph{Toward the full conjecture.} Theorem~\ref{thm:struct} reduces real-rootedness of all $B_n$ to compatibility of $(A_j)$, which follows from mutual interlacing propagated by the Eulerian recurrence. The missing ingredient is a formal theory of interlacing/compatible polynomials (sign patterns at roots, the common-interlacer lemma, closure under nonnegative combinations); establishing that the recurrence preserves interlacing would upgrade Theorem~\ref{thm:rr} to all $n$.

\paragraph{Higher powers.} For the $m$-th power $T^m$, the row polynomials satisfy $B^{(m)}_n=\sum_j A(n,j)B^{(m-1)}_j$ (with $B^{(1)}_j=A_j$). Once compatibility is available, an induction on $m$ would give real-rootedness of all rows of all powers.

\paragraph{Uniform brackets.} A purely elementary route to all $n$ needs a uniform family of rational separating brackets; none is known, since the smallest roots cluster toward $0$ as $n\to\infty$.

\paragraph{Coefficient structure.} Proposition~\ref{prop:shape} should be promoted to general lemmas (degree $n-2$, monic, positive coefficients, constant term $n!$) to streamline the induction.

\paragraph{Related triangles.} The same strategy applies to squares and powers of the Pascal, Stirling, and Narayana triangles, whose row polynomials are also real-rooted.

\section{Conclusion}
The square of the Eulerian triangle carries rigid analytic structure: its row polynomials are nonnegative Eulerian-weighted combinations of Eulerian polynomials (Theorem~\ref{thm:struct}); all their real roots are strictly negative unconditionally (Theorem~\ref{thm:neg}); and they are genuinely real-rooted at least up to $n=10$ (Theorems~\ref{thm:rr}--\ref{thm:rrneg}), past the integer-bracket obstruction at $n=8$. The path to the full conjecture and to arbitrary powers runs through a formal theory of interlacing polynomials, which the structural identity places within reach.

